# Implement report — etude-n9b.2

Implemented the approved opt-in checkout-read policy for live gate model seats.

ANCESTOR: `etude-n9b` (P1) delivers GitHub issue #16 without changing the
output-only default. MEASUREMENT: before this change every live model seat ran
with the checkout as its working directory but received no explicit access
policy or measurement-authority instruction, so a reviewer could inspect code
or recompute embedded measurements without an auditable workflow grant.

BLAST RADIUS: repository worktree only. The implementation creates no durable
external state, host configuration, service, package, privilege, or large
per-run artifact. An opted-in live gate creates one temporary detached worktree
and removes it when gate execution returns.

## Behavior

- Live gates resolve `read_checkout` from the current workflow only when model
  seats exist, create a dedicated detached seat checkout at the pinned SHA, and
  record the effective grant in each `GateAttempt`.
- All model seats receive one byte-identical shared prompt. The default says
  `OUTPUT-ONLY` and forbids checkout inspection. The opt-in says `READ-ONLY`
  and names the run's pinned commit.
- Every seat prompt says embedded provenance is AUTHORITATIVE for measurements,
  prohibits re-derived arithmetic, restricts reads to falsifying claims, and
  requires BLOCK with evidence instead of silently substituted numbers.
- Deterministic checks retain the raw stage output input rather than receiving
  the model review prompt. They continue in the stage worktree and cannot mutate
  the separate checkout presented to opted-in model seats.
- Supervised gates reject `read_checkout` before resolving checks or seats
  because their caller tree can be mutable and uncommitted.
- While GitHub issue #14 remains open, live read grants recursively inspect the
  pinned git tree and fail closed before invoking seats when an actual
  mode-160000 gitlink is present. A stray `.gitmodules` file is not treated as
  a submodule; output-only behavior is unchanged.

